{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Jacob's firstborn, Reuben.</b> He did not lose the birthright in the laws of Heaven, on account of the repentance that reaches the Throne of Glory; nor did he lose it even in this world until his father Jacob deposed him by human law — as is the case with anyone liable to excommunication, who is not actually excommunicated until a sage of the generation excommunicates him, as they said, of blessed memory (Mo'ed Katan 16a): Whence do we know that we excommunicate? For it is written אוֹרוּ מֵרוֹז (Judges 5:23) — for that important man thought so, for it is written אָמַר מַלְאַךְ ה'. And so the later authorities ruled."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "Sforno quotes the Talmudic passage in Aramaic; the phrases 'for that important man thought so' and 'for it is written' are retained as he gives them, in the Aramaic-derived idiom of the original Talmudic discussion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:33:28.703378814Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}